{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-26T11:30:24Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nFractures of the Upper limb —\nGeneral causes\n\n* Fall on an outstretched hand (FOOSH)\n* Direct blow or trauma\n* Road-traffic accidents\n* Sports injuries\n* Twisting or rotational injuries\n* Occupational injuries\n* Osteoporosis/pathological bone weakness\n\nGeneral clinical features\n\n* Pain and tenderness\n* Swelling and bruising\n* Deformity or abnormal movement\n* Loss of function and restricted ROM\n* Crepitus\n* Shortening or abnormal alignment\n* Neurovascular deficit in complicated fractures\n\nGeneral complications\n\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Infection in open fractures\n* Delayed union/non-union\n* Malunion\n* Joint stiffness\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n* Complex regional pain syndrome\n\n⸻\n\n1. Fractures of Clavicle and Scapula\n\nA. Fracture of clavicle\n\nDefinition:\nFracture of the clavicle, most commonly involving the middle third.\n\nCauses\n\n* Fall directly on the shoulder\n* Fall on an outstretched hand\n* Direct blow to clavicle\n* Road-traffic or sports injury\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain and tenderness over clavicle\n* Swelling and deformity\n* Patient supports the affected arm\n* Shoulder drooping forwards and downwards\n* Abnormal mobility/crepitus\n* Difficulty in shoulder movements\n* Check distal neurovascular status\n\nComplications\n\n* Malunion\n* Non-union\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Skin perforation\n* Brachial plexus injury\n* Pneumothorax\n* Shoulder stiffness\n\nConservative management\n\n* Arm sling or figure-of-eight brace\n* Analgesics\n* Ice/elevation initially\n* Early elbow, wrist and hand movements\n* Gradual shoulder ROM after pain subsides\n\nSurgical management\n\n* ORIF with plate and screws\n* Indications: open fracture, neurovascular injury, significant displacement/shortening, threatened skin, or selected unstable displaced fractures.\n\nB. Fracture of scapula\n\nCauses\n\n* Usually high-energy trauma\n* Direct blow\n* RTA\n* Fall from height\n* Indirect injury through shoulder\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe shoulder/scapular pain\n* Swelling and tenderness\n* Painful shoulder movements\n* Reduced shoulder ROM\n* Associated chest injuries may be present\n\nComplications\n\n* Associated rib and chest injuries\n* Pneumothorax\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Malunion\n* Shoulder stiffness\n* Glenohumeral instability\n\nManagement\n\n* Conservative: sling, analgesia, rest followed by early ROM.\n* Surgical: ORIF for significantly displaced body/neck fractures, intra-articular glenoid fractures, open fractures or fractures associated with instability. \n\n⸻\n\n2. Fractures of Greater Tuberosity and Neck of Humerus\n\nA. Greater tuberosity fracture\n\nCauses\n\n* Fall on shoulder/FOOSH\n* Shoulder dislocation\n* Direct trauma\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain and tenderness over greater tuberosity\n* Swelling\n* Painful and restricted abduction\n* Weakness of rotator cuff\n* Difficulty raising arm\n\nComplications\n\n* Malunion\n* Shoulder stiffness\n* Rotator cuff dysfunction\n* Subacromial impingement\n* Non-union\n\nManagement\n\n* Conservative: sling, analgesics and early passive ROM when appropriate.\n* Surgical: ORIF for significantly displaced fragments, particularly when displacement causes impingement or affects shoulder mechanics.\n\nB. Fracture of neck of humerus\n\nCauses\n\n* FOOSH\n* Fall directly on shoulder\n* Osteoporotic fall in elderly\n* High-energy trauma in young adults\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain and swelling around shoulder\n* Tenderness\n* Inability to move shoulder\n* Bruising\n* Deformity\n* Check axillary nerve and distal circulation\n\nComplications\n\n* Axillary nerve injury\n* Vascular injury\n* Avascular necrosis of humeral head\n* Malunion/non-union\n* Shoulder stiffness\n* Rotator cuff dysfunction\n\nManagement\n\n* Conservative: sling/shoulder immobilizer, analgesia and progressive ROM for undisplaced/stable fractures.\n* Surgical: ORIF or arthroplasty for displaced, unstable, comminuted or selected fracture-dislocations. \n\n⸻\n\n3. Fracture Shaft of Humerus\n\nDefinition:\nFracture of the humeral diaphysis between the surgical neck and supracondylar region.\n\nCauses\n\n* Fall\n* Direct blow\n* RTA\n* Sports injury\n* Pathological fracture\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain and swelling of arm\n* Deformity\n* Abnormal mobility and crepitus\n* Loss of arm function\n* Radial nerve palsy: wrist drop, inability to extend wrist/fingers and sensory changes over the dorsum of hand\n\nComplications\n\n* Radial nerve injury\n* Brachial artery injury\n* Non-union\n* Malunion\n* Infection\n* Shoulder/elbow stiffness\n\nConservative management\n\n* Coaptation splint or hanging arm cast initially\n* Functional brace after swelling decreases\n* Analgesia\n* Maintain elbow, wrist and hand movements\n* Regular radiographic follow-up\n\nSurgical management\n\n* ORIF with plate and screws\n* Intramedullary nailing\n* External fixation in selected open/severe injuries\n* Indications include open fracture, vascular injury, multiple injuries, unacceptable alignment, pathological fracture and failure of conservative treatment. \n\n⸻\n\n4. Supracondylar Fracture of Humerus\n\nDefinition:\nFracture of the distal humerus just above the elbow condyles, commonly seen in children.\n\nCauses\n\n* Fall on outstretched hand\n* Fall onto flexed elbow\n* Sports injury\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe elbow pain\n* Swelling and deformity\n* Loss of elbow movement\n* Tenderness\n* Abnormal carrying angle\n* Assess brachial artery and median, radial and ulnar nerves\n\nComplications\n\n* Brachial artery injury\n* Median/anterior interosseous nerve injury\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Volkmann ischaemic contracture\n* Cubitus varus\n* Elbow stiffness\n* Malunion\n\nConservative management\n\n* Gartland type I: above-elbow cast/splint with elbow flexed appropriately and neurovascular monitoring.\n* Regular X-ray follow-up.\n* Avoid excessive elbow flexion because it may compromise circulation. \n\nSurgical management\n\n* Closed reduction and percutaneous pinning (CRPP) for displaced/unstable fractures.\n* Open reduction and internal fixation when closed reduction fails or in selected complicated fractures.\n* Urgent surgery if there is vascular compromise or an open injury.\n\n⸻\n\n5. Side-Swipe Injury of Elbow\n\nDefinition:\nA side-swipe injury is a high-energy injury to the elbow produced by a direct blow, classically when the elbow is struck by a passing vehicle.\n\nCauses\n\n* Direct high-energy blow to the elbow\n* Road-traffic accident\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe pain and swelling\n* Open wound commonly present\n* Deformity around elbow\n* Restricted elbow movement\n* Fracture-dislocation may occur\n* Possible vascular and nerve injury\n\nComplications\n\n* Infection\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Elbow stiffness\n* Non-union/malunion\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n\nConservative management\n\n* Usually limited because these are commonly high-energy, unstable injuries.\n* Initial splintage\n* Analgesia\n* Wound care\n* Antibiotics and tetanus prophylaxis for open injuries\n* Neurovascular monitoring\n\nSurgical management\n\n* Urgent irrigation and debridement for open injury\n* Reduction of dislocation\n* ORIF of associated fractures\n* External fixation when there is severe soft-tissue injury or instability.\n\n⸻\n\n6. Fractures of Radius and Ulna\n\nBoth-bone forearm fracture\n\nDefinition:\nFracture of both the radius and ulna, usually involving their shafts.\n\nCauses\n\n* Direct blow\n* FOOSH\n* RTA\n* Sports injury\n* Twisting injury\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain and swelling\n* Deformity and shortening\n* Abnormal movement/crepitus\n* Loss of pronation and supination\n* Neurovascular assessment is essential\n\nComplications\n\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Malunion\n* Non-union\n* Loss of pronation/supination\n* Nerve and vascular injury\n* Infection\n* Radioulnar synostosis\n\nManagement\n\n* Conservative: closed reduction and above-elbow cast in selected children/undisplaced fractures.\n* Surgical: ORIF with plates and screws is commonly required in adults because accurate restoration of radial bow and forearm rotation is important.\n\nChauffeur fracture\n\nImportant correction: Chauffeur fracture is NOT a fracture of both radius and ulna. It is an intra-articular fracture of the radial styloid process. \n\nManagement\n\n* Undisplaced: splint/cast.\n* Displaced or unstable: reduction and fixation, commonly ORIF.\n\n⸻\n\n7. Fractures of Capitulum, Radial Head and Olecranon\n\nA. Capitulum fracture\n\nCauses\n\n* FOOSH\n* Direct trauma\n* Elbow dislocation\n\nClinical features\n\n* Elbow pain and swelling\n* Tenderness over lateral elbow\n* Restricted flexion-extension\n* Mechanical locking may occur\n\nComplications\n\n* Non-union\n* Malunion\n* Elbow stiffness\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n\nManagement\n\n* Undisplaced: immobilization followed by early ROM.\n* Displaced: ORIF with screws; excision of small non-reconstructable fragments may be considered in selected cases.\n\nB. Radial head fracture\n\nCauses\n\n* FOOSH\n* Valgus force at elbow\n* Direct trauma\n\nClinical features\n\n* Lateral elbow pain\n* Tenderness over radial head\n* Painful pronation and supination\n* Restricted elbow movement\n* Elbow effusion\n\nComplications\n\n* Elbow stiffness\n* Non-union\n* Malunion\n* Instability\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n\nManagement\n\n* Undisplaced: sling briefly followed by early movement.\n* Displaced/unstable: ORIF; radial head replacement may be required for severely comminuted fractures.\n\nC. Olecranon fracture\n\nCauses\n\n* Direct fall on elbow\n* Triceps contraction\n* RTA\n\nClinical features\n\n* Posterior elbow pain and swelling\n* Tenderness\n* Palpable fracture gap\n* Inability to actively extend elbow in displaced fractures\n\nComplications\n\n* Non-union\n* Elbow stiffness\n* Loss of extension\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n* Ulnar nerve symptoms\n\nManagement\n\n* Undisplaced and stable: above-elbow splint/cast with monitoring.\n* Displaced: ORIF using tension-band wiring or plate fixation. \n\n⸻\n\n8. Monteggia and Galeazzi Fractures\n\nA. Monteggia fracture-dislocation\n\nDefinition:\nFracture of the proximal or middle ulna associated with dislocation of the radial head at the radiocapitellar joint. \n\nCauses\n\n* FOOSH\n* Direct blow\n* RTA\n* Hyperpronation injury\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain and swelling of forearm/elbow\n* Deformity\n* Restricted elbow movement\n* Prominent/displaced radial head\n* Possible posterior interosseous nerve palsy\n\nComplications\n\n* Radial/PIN injury\n* Malunion\n* Non-union\n* Elbow stiffness\n* Chronic radial-head dislocation\n* Compartment syndrome\n\nManagement\n\n* Children: closed reduction and cast immobilization may be successful when stable.\n* Adults/unstable injuries: ORIF of ulna, followed by reduction and assessment of radial head stability. \n\nB. Galeazzi fracture-dislocation\n\nDefinition:\nFracture of the middle/distal third of the radial shaft with disruption or dislocation of the distal radioulnar joint (DRUJ). \n\nCauses\n\n* FOOSH with hyperpronation\n* RTA\n* High-energy trauma\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain and swelling of forearm/wrist\n* Radial deformity\n* Prominent ulnar head\n* Restricted pronation and supination\n* DRUJ tenderness/instability\n\nComplications\n\n* DRUJ instability\n* Malunion\n* Non-union\n* Loss of forearm rotation\n* Chronic wrist pain\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n\nManagement\n\n* Children: closed reduction and long-arm immobilization may be used when stable.\n* Adults: ORIF of radius with plate and screws; DRUJ stability is assessed after fixation and stabilized if necessary. \n\n⸻\n\n9. Fractures of Wrist and Hand Complex – Smith’s Fracture\n\nSmith’s fracture\n\nDefinition:\nSmith’s fracture is a fracture of the distal radius with volar displacement/angulation of the distal fragment, often considered the reverse of a typical Colles’ fracture.\n\nCauses\n\n* Fall onto the dorsum of the flexed wrist\n* Direct blow to the back of the wrist\n* RTA\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain and swelling around wrist\n* Tenderness over distal radius\n* Volar deformity\n* Restricted wrist and finger movements\n* Possible median nerve symptoms\n* Reduced grip strength\n\nComplications\n\n* Malunion\n* Median nerve compression/carpal tunnel syndrome\n* Wrist stiffness\n* Tendon problems\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n* Complex regional pain syndrome\n\nConservative management\n\n* Closed reduction\n* Below-elbow or above-elbow cast depending on stability\n* Elevation and analgesics\n* Regular X-rays to detect loss of reduction\n* Finger and later wrist ROM exercises\n\nSurgical management\n\n* ORIF, commonly with a volar locking plate, for displaced/unstable or intra-articular fractures.\n* External fixation may be used in selected severe comminuted injuries.///////////////////////Dislocations of the Upper Limb —-\n\nDefinition\n\nDislocation is the complete loss of normal articular contact between the articular surfaces of a joint due to trauma or other causes.\n\nThe shoulder is the most commonly dislocated major joint, with anterior dislocation being the most common type. \n\n⸻\n\n1. Shoulder Dislocation\n\nMechanism of injury\n\n* Anterior dislocation: Fall on an outstretched arm or forceful combination of abduction, extension and external rotation.\n* Posterior dislocation: Direct blow to the anterior shoulder with the arm adducted and internally rotated.\n* Violent muscle contraction, such as during seizures or electrical injury, may cause posterior dislocation. \n\n⸻\n\nA. Anterior dislocation of shoulder\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe pain following trauma\n* Patient supports the affected arm\n* Arm held in abduction and external rotation\n* Loss of normal rounded contour of shoulder\n* Prominent acromion, producing a squared-off shoulder\n* Humeral head may be palpable anteriorly\n* Complete restriction of active and passive movements\n* Check axillary nerve and distal vascular status\n\nComplications\n\n* Axillary nerve injury\n* Axillary artery injury\n* Greater tuberosity fracture\n* Hill-Sachs lesion\n* Bankart lesion\n* Rotator cuff injury\n* Recurrent dislocation\n* Joint stiffness\n\nAxillary nerve injury is particularly important and should be assessed before and after reduction. \n\n⸻\n\nB. Posterior dislocation of shoulder\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe shoulder pain\n* Arm held in adduction and internal rotation\n* Inability to externally rotate the arm\n* Flattening of the anterior shoulder\n* Prominent posterior humeral head may be palpable\n* Restricted shoulder movements\n\nComplications\n\n* Reverse Hill-Sachs lesion\n* Fracture of surgical neck or tuberosity\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Rotator cuff injury\n* Recurrent instability\n* Osteoarthritis\n\nPosterior dislocation is uncommon and may be missed clinically, particularly when the patient presents with a guarded internally rotated arm. \n\n⸻\n\nConservative Management of Shoulder Dislocation\n\n1. Reduction\n\nThe dislocation is reduced by closed manipulation, preferably with adequate analgesia/sedation and muscle relaxation.\n\nKocher’s manoeuvre\n\n1. Patient supine.\n2. Arm adducted to the side.\n3. Elbow flexed to 90°.\n4. Slowly externally rotate the arm until resistance is felt.\n5. Elevate/flex the arm while maintaining external rotation.\n6. Adduct the arm.\n7. Internally rotate the shoulder to complete reduction. \n\nNote: Forceful Kocher manoeuvre should be avoided because it can cause fractures and soft-tissue injury.\n\nHippocrates manoeuvre\n\n1. Patient lies supine.\n2. The affected arm is gently grasped.\n3. Axillary counter-traction is applied.\n4. Longitudinal traction is applied to the limb.\n5. Gentle manipulation is continued until the humeral head returns to the glenoid.\n\nThe classical Hippocratic technique is now generally not preferred because of the risk of brachial plexus and vascular injury. \n\nAfter reduction\n\n* Confirm reduction clinically and radiologically.\n* Reassess neurovascular status.\n* Apply sling/immobilizer.\n* Analgesics.\n* Gradual ROM exercises.\n* Physiotherapy for rotator-cuff and scapular-muscle strengthening.\n\n⸻\n\nSurgical Management\n\nIndications\n\n* Irreducible dislocation\n* Recurrent dislocation\n* Associated fracture\n* Significant bone loss\n* Persistent instability\n* Major labral injury\n* Failed conservative treatment\n\nImportant operations\n\n1. Bankart repair\n\n* Repair of the detached anteroinferior glenoid labrum and capsule.\n* May be performed arthroscopically or by open technique.\n* Common procedure for recurrent anterior instability.\n\n2. Putti-Platt operation\n\n* A historical/non-anatomic stabilization procedure.\n* Involves shortening and tightening the subscapularis and anterior capsule.\n* Reduces external rotation and therefore limits instability.\n* It has largely been replaced by anatomic Bankart repair in modern practice. \n\n3. Latarjet procedure\n\n* Coracoid transfer to the anterior glenoid.\n* Used particularly when there is significant glenoid bone loss or recurrent instability.\n\n⸻\n\n2. Recurrent Dislocation of Shoulder\n\nDefinition\n\nRepeated episodes of shoulder dislocation or subluxation following an initial traumatic dislocation.\n\nCauses\n\n* Inadequate healing of capsule/labrum\n* Bankart lesion\n* Glenoid bone loss\n* Hill-Sachs lesion\n* Ligamentous laxity\n* Inadequate rehabilitation\n\nClinical features\n\n* Repeated episodes of dislocation\n* Apprehension during abduction and external rotation\n* Pain and instability\n* Reduced confidence in shoulder movements\n* Positive apprehension test\n\nManagement\n\nConservative\n\n* Activity modification\n* Shoulder immobilization when indicated\n* Physiotherapy\n* Rotator-cuff strengthening\n* Scapular stabilization exercises\n* Proprioceptive training\n\nSurgical\n\n* Arthroscopic/open Bankart repair\n* Putti-Platt procedure\n* Latarjet procedure\n* Remplissage or other bone/soft-tissue procedures depending on associated lesions.\n\nRecurrent instability is particularly common in young active patients after traumatic anterior dislocation. \n\n⸻\n\n3. Dislocation of Elbow\n\nMechanism of injury\n\n* Commonly due to fall on an outstretched hand\n* Hyperextension and valgus/varus forces\n* Sports injuries\n* Road-traffic accidents\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe pain\n* Swelling\n* Obvious deformity\n* Loss of elbow movement\n* Prominent olecranon or abnormal relationship of bony landmarks\n* Neurovascular assessment is essential\n\nComplications\n\n* Brachial artery injury\n* Median, ulnar or radial nerve injury\n* Fracture associated with dislocation\n* Elbow stiffness\n* Chronic instability\n* Heterotopic ossification\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n\nConservative management\n\n1. Closed reduction under adequate analgesia/sedation.\n2. Confirm reduction with X-ray.\n3. Short period of immobilization.\n4. Early active ROM once stability permits.\n5. Physiotherapy.\n\nEarly mobilization is important because prolonged immobilization increases the risk of elbow stiffness and poor functional outcome. \n\nSurgical management\n\n* Open reduction if irreducible.\n* Repair/reconstruction of collateral ligaments in unstable injuries.\n* Fixation of associated fractures.\n* External fixation for severe instability when required.\n\n⸻\n\n4. Hand Injuries\n\nA. Crush Injuries\n\nDefinition\n\nInjury caused by compression of the hand between two objects, resulting in damage to skin, muscles, tendons, nerves, vessels and bones.\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain and swelling\n* Bruising\n* Skin and soft-tissue damage\n* Fracture/dislocation\n* Loss of movement\n* Sensory loss\n* Vascular compromise\n\nComplications\n\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Tissue necrosis\n* Infection\n* Tendon adhesions\n* Nerve injury\n* Stiffness\n* Contracture\n* Amputation\n\nManagement\n\nConservative\n\n* Elevation\n* Analgesia\n* Wound dressing\n* Splintage\n* Antibiotics when indicated\n* Early controlled ROM when safe\n\nSurgical\n\n* Thorough wound exploration and debridement\n* Repair of tendons, nerves and vessels\n* Fracture fixation\n* Fasciotomy for compartment syndrome\n* Skin graft/flap coverage\n* Amputation when tissue is non-viable.\n\n⸻\n\n5. Flexor Tendon Injuries\n\nCauses\n\n* Sharp cuts\n* Glass injuries\n* Crush injuries\n* Lacerations\n\nClinical features\n\n* Loss of active finger flexion\n* Open wound\n* Altered resting finger position\n* Loss of grip\n* Sensory or vascular injury may coexist\n\nManagement\n\nConservative\n\n* Wound care and splintage in selected partial injuries.\n* Early protected rehabilitation after repair.\n\nSurgical\n\n* Primary tendon repair for complete lacerations when appropriate.\n* Secondary tendon reconstruction if primary repair is not possible.\n* Postoperative controlled tendon-gliding exercises and splinting.\n\n⸻\n\n6. Extensor Tendon Injuries\n\nCauses\n\n* Laceration\n* Crush injury\n* Direct trauma\n* Closed tendon rupture\n\nClinical features\n\n* Loss of active finger extension\n* Finger deformity depending on level of injury\n* Weak grip\n* Open wound in lacerations\n\nComplications\n\n* Adhesions\n* Extensor lag\n* Rupture of repair\n* Joint stiffness\n* Deformity\n\nManagement\n\nConservative\n\n* Splinting in appropriate position for selected injuries.\n* Controlled mobilization.\n\nSurgical\n\n* Tendon repair for appropriate complete lacerations.\n* Tendon graft or transfer for chronic injuries.\n* Postoperative splinting and rehabilitation.\n\n⸻\n\n7. Burn Injuries of Hand\n\nDefinition\n\nBurn injury is tissue damage caused by thermal, chemical, electrical or other energy sources.\n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain\n* Erythema\n* Blistering\n* Swelling\n* Loss of skin and soft tissue in deep burns\n* Reduced ROM\n* Circumferential burns may compromise circulation.\n\nComplications\n\n* Infection\n* Edema\n* Skin and tendon damage\n* Hypertrophic scar\n* Contracture\n* Web-space contracture\n* Burn claw deformity\n* Loss of finger movement\n* Functional disability\n\nConservative management\n\n1. Assess burn depth and extent.\n2. Clean and dress the wound.\n3. Elevate the hand to reduce edema.\n4. Maintain adequate analgesia.\n5. Splint in anti-deformity/intrinsic-plus position:\n    * Wrist: approximately 20–30° extension\n    * MCP joints: 70–90° flexion\n    * IP joints: extension\n    * Thumb: abducted\n6. Early supervised ROM when safe.\n7. Scar management and pressure therapy during healing.\n8. Physiotherapy/occupational therapy.\n\nThese measures are important because edema, scarring and prolonged immobilization can rapidly destroy hand function, which humans apparently decided was worth preserving only after discovering fire. \n\nSurgical management\n\n* Escharotomy for circumferential burns with vascular compromise.\n* Early excision/debridement of deep burns.\n* Skin grafting for deep partial-thickness and full-thickness burns.\n* Flap reconstruction when tendons, joints or other deep structures are exposed.\n* Scar release and grafting/flap procedures for established contractures.////////////////////Fractures of the Lower Limb —\n\nGeneral causes\n\n* Road-traffic accidents\n* Fall from height or fall on the limb\n* Direct blow\n* Twisting injuries\n* Sports injuries\n* Osteoporosis/pathological conditions\n\nGeneral clinical features\n\n* Pain and tenderness\n* Swelling and bruising\n* Deformity and shortening\n* Loss of function\n* Abnormal mobility and crepitus\n* Restricted joint movements\n* Neurovascular deficit in complicated injuries\n\nGeneral complications\n\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Infection in open fractures\n* Delayed union/non-union\n* Malunion\n* Joint stiffness\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n* Fat embolism, particularly with long-bone fractures\n\n⸻\n\n1. FRACTURE NECK OF FEMUR\n\nDefinition\n\nFracture of the femoral neck occurring between the femoral head and trochanteric region. It is an intracapsular fracture and may compromise the blood supply of the femoral head. \n\nCauses\n\n* Fall in elderly patients, commonly with osteoporosis\n* Direct trauma\n* Road-traffic accidents\n* Fall from height\n* Pathological weakening of bone\n\nClassification\n\nA. Anatomical classification\n\n1. Subcapital – immediately below femoral head\n2. Transcervical – through middle of neck\n3. Basicervical – at junction of neck and trochanters \n\nB. Garden classification\n\n* Type I: Incomplete/valgus impacted fracture\n* Type II: Complete but undisplaced fracture\n* Type III: Complete fracture with partial displacement\n* Type IV: Complete fracture with complete displacement\n\nC. Pauwels classification\n\n* Type I: Fracture angle <30°\n* Type II: 30–50°\n* Type III: >50° and most vertically oriented/unstable. \n\nClinical features\n\n* Pain in groin/hip\n* Inability to bear weight\n* Limb appears shortened\n* Limb lies in external rotation\n* Tenderness around hip\n* Pain on hip movement\n* In impacted fractures, the patient may still be able to walk\n\nComplications\n\n* Avascular necrosis of femoral head\n* Non-union\n* Malunion\n* Infection\n* Implant/fixation failure\n* Thromboembolism\n* Hip stiffness\n* Post-traumatic osteoarthritis \n\nConservative management\n\nReserved mainly for patients who are medically unfit for surgery or selected stable/impacted fractures.\n\n* Bed rest initially\n* Analgesics\n* Skin traction for pain relief when indicated\n* Prevention of pressure sores and DVT\n* Chest physiotherapy\n* Early mobilization with walking aids as tolerated\n* Regular radiographic follow-up\n\nAO notes that non-operative treatment is generally reserved for medically infirm patients who cannot tolerate surgery. \n\nSurgical management\n\nUndisplaced/impacted fractures\n\n* Percutaneous cannulated cancellous screws\n* Femoral neck system in selected cases\n\nDisplaced fractures\n\nYoung patients:\n\n* Urgent reduction\n* Internal fixation with cannulated screws/DHS/FNS depending on fracture pattern\n\nElderly patients:\n\n* Hemiarthroplasty\n* Total hip arthroplasty in selected active patients\n\nCurrent AO guidance includes cannulated screws, femoral neck system, hemiarthroplasty and total hip arthroplasty according to fracture pattern and patient factors. \n\n⸻\n\n2. FRACTURES OF TROCHANTERS\n\nDefinition\n\nFractures involving the greater and/or lesser trochanter, commonly including intertrochanteric fractures.\n\nCauses\n\n* Fall in elderly patients\n* Direct trauma\n* RTA\n* Fall from height\n* Osteoporosis\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe hip pain\n* Inability to bear weight\n* Swelling and tenderness over hip\n* Shortening of limb\n* External rotation\n* Painful hip movements\n* Bruising around hip/thigh\n\nComplications\n\n* Blood loss\n* Malunion with varus deformity\n* Non-union\n* Limb shortening\n* Implant failure\n* DVT and pulmonary embolism\n* Infection\n* Hip stiffness\n\nConservative management\n\nUsed only in selected patients who are unsuitable for surgery.\n\n* Bed rest\n* Analgesics\n* Traction for selected cases\n* Prevention of pressure sores and DVT\n* Physiotherapy\n* Gradual mobilization with walking aids\n\nSurgical management\n\nPreferred in most medically fit patients.\n\n* Dynamic hip screw (DHS)\n* Proximal femoral nail (PFN)\n* Cephalomedullary nail\n* Dynamic condylar screw in selected patterns\n* Trochanteric stabilization plate for selected unstable fractures\n\nAO describes sliding hip screw, trochanteric stabilization and intramedullary nailing among operative options for trochanteric fractures. \n\n⸻\n\n3. FRACTURE SHAFT OF FEMUR\n\nDefinition\n\nFracture involving the diaphysis of the femur, extending between the lesser trochanter and the distal metaphyseal region.\n\nMechanism of injury\n\n* High-energy trauma: RTA, fall from height\n* Direct blow\n* Twisting force\n* Pathological fracture\n* Low-energy injury in osteoporotic elderly patients\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe thigh pain\n* Swelling\n* Deformity\n* Shortening\n* Abnormal mobility\n* Crepitus\n* Inability to bear weight\n* Significant blood loss may occur\n* Assess distal neurovascular status\n\nComplications\n\n* Hemorrhagic shock\n* Fat embolism syndrome\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Infection\n* Delayed union\n* Non-union\n* Malunion\n* Knee/hip stiffness\n\nConservative management\n\nUsed mainly in selected children or patients unsuitable for surgery.\n\n* Skin/skeletal traction\n* Manipulation and reduction\n* Functional bracing in selected cases\n* Analgesics\n* Physiotherapy\n* Gradual weight bearing after union\n\nSurgical management\n\nPreferred in most adult femoral shaft fractures.\n\n* Intramedullary interlocking nail: commonly preferred\n* ORIF with plate and screws in selected cases\n* External fixation for severe open fractures/polytrauma\n* Early mobilization and rehabilitation\n\nAO identifies antegrade nailing and bridge plating among established methods for femoral shaft fractures. \n\n⸻\n\n4. SUPRACONDYLAR FRACTURE OF FEMUR\n\nDefinition\n\nFracture of the distal femur occurring just above the femoral condyles.\n\nCauses\n\n* High-energy RTA\n* Fall from height\n* Direct trauma\n* Osteoporosis in elderly\n* Pathological fracture\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe knee/thigh pain\n* Swelling\n* Deformity\n* Shortening\n* Inability to bear weight\n* Restricted knee movement\n* Possible open wound\n* Neurovascular assessment is essential\n\nComplications\n\n* Popliteal artery injury\n* Nerve injury\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Malunion\n* Non-union\n* Knee stiffness\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n* Infection\n\nConservative management\n\nFor selected undisplaced fractures or patients unsuitable for surgery:\n\n* Above-knee cast/brace\n* Traction when indicated\n* Analgesia\n* Regular X-rays\n* Quadriceps exercises\n* Gradual knee ROM and weight bearing\n\nSurgical management\n\n* ORIF with locking compression plate\n* Retrograde intramedullary nail\n* External fixation in selected open/complex injuries\n* Bone grafting for selected non-unions\n\n⸻\n\n5. FRACTURES OF THE CONDYLES OF FEMUR\n\nDefinition\n\nFractures involving the medial or lateral femoral condyle, which may extend into the knee joint.\n\nCauses\n\n* High-energy trauma\n* Direct blow\n* Fall\n* Twisting injury\n* Osteoporosis\n\nClinical features\n\n* Knee pain\n* Swelling and haemarthrosis\n* Tenderness over affected condyle\n* Restricted knee movement\n* Inability to bear weight\n* Deformity in displaced fractures\n\nComplications\n\n* Knee stiffness\n* Malunion\n* Non-union\n* Joint incongruity\n* Post-traumatic osteoarthritis\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Infection\n\nConservative management\n\nFor undisplaced and stable fractures:\n\n* Above-knee immobilization\n* Analgesics\n* Elevation\n* Regular radiographs\n* Quadriceps exercises\n* Gradual ROM and weight bearing\n\nSurgical management\n\nFor displaced/intra-articular fractures:\n\n* Open reduction and internal fixation\n* Lag screws\n* Buttress/locking plates\n* Bone grafting when required\n* Early controlled knee movement after stable fixation\n\n⸻\n\n6. FRACTURE PATELLA\n\nDefinition\n\nFracture of the patella, commonly caused by direct trauma or sudden forceful contraction of the quadriceps.\n\nCauses\n\n* Direct fall onto knee\n* Direct blow\n* RTA\n* Sudden forceful quadriceps contraction\n\nTypes\n\n* Transverse\n* Vertical\n* Comminuted\n* Marginal\n* Osteochondral\n\nClinical features\n\n* Anterior knee pain\n* Swelling and haemarthrosis\n* Tenderness\n* Bruising\n* Inability to extend knee actively in displaced fractures\n* Palpable gap between fragments\n\nComplications\n\n* Non-union\n* Malunion\n* Extensor lag\n* Knee stiffness\n* Post-traumatic arthritis\n* Quadriceps weakness\n* Infection after surgery\n\nConservative management\n\nFor undisplaced fractures with intact extensor mechanism:\n\n* Knee immobilizer/above-knee cast\n* Knee in extension\n* Analgesia\n* Quadriceps exercises\n* Gradual ROM\n* Progressive weight bearing\n\nSurgical management\n\nIndicated for displaced fractures or disruption of the extensor mechanism.\n\n* ORIF with tension-band wiring\n* Cannulated screws with tension band\n* Plate fixation for comminuted fractures\n* Partial patellectomy for selected non-reconstructable inferior/superior pole fragments\n* Total patellectomy only in exceptional severely comminuted cases\n\n⸻\n\n7. FRACTURES OF TIBIAL CONDYLES\n\nDefinition\n\nFractures involving the proximal tibial articular surface, also called tibial plateau fractures.\n\nCauses\n\n* RTA\n* Fall from height\n* Sports injuries\n* Valgus/varus stress\n* Axial loading\n* Osteoporosis\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe knee pain\n* Swelling\n* Haemarthrosis\n* Tenderness over tibial plateau\n* Inability to bear weight\n* Restricted knee movements\n* Deformity\n* Possible ligament/meniscal injury\n\nComplications\n\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Knee stiffness\n* Malunion\n* Non-union\n* Post-traumatic osteoarthritis\n* Ligament instability\n* Infection\n\nConservative management\n\nFor undisplaced/minimally displaced stable fractures:\n\n* Knee immobilization/hinged brace\n* Ice and elevation\n* Analgesics\n* Non-weight bearing\n* Quadriceps and ankle exercises\n* Gradual ROM\n* Progressive weight bearing after radiological healing\n\nSurgical management\n\nFor displaced, unstable or significantly depressed articular fractures:\n\n* Closed reduction and percutaneous fixation in selected cases\n* ORIF with plates and screws\n* Elevation of depressed articular surface\n* Bone graft/substitute where required\n* External fixation for severe soft-tissue injury/high-energy fractures\n\nAO includes ORIF with conventional or locking plate techniques among operative methods for proximal tibial fractures. \n\n⸻\n\n8. BOTH-BONE FRACTURE OF TIBIA AND FIBULA\n\nDefinition\n\nFracture of both the tibial and fibular shafts, usually caused by significant trauma.\n\nCauses\n\n* RTA\n* Direct blow\n* Fall from height\n* Sports injury\n* Twisting injury\n* Crush injury\n\nMechanism\n\n* Direct bending force\n* Indirect twisting force\n* Combination of bending and rotational forces\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe pain\n* Swelling\n* Deformity and shortening\n* Abnormal mobility\n* Crepitus\n* Inability to bear weight\n* Open wound may be present\n* Neurovascular deficit may occur\n\nComplications\n\n* Compartment syndrome\n* Infection, particularly in open fractures\n* Delayed union\n* Non-union\n* Malunion\n* Limb shortening\n* Neurovascular injury\n* Knee/ankle stiffness\n* Chronic osteomyelitis\n\nConservative management\n\nFor selected stable/undisplaced fractures:\n\n* Closed reduction\n* Above-knee cast initially\n* Below-knee cast after adequate stability/union as appropriate\n* Elevation and analgesia\n* Regular X-rays\n* Non-weight bearing followed by progressive weight bearing\n* Physiotherapy\n\nSurgical management\n\nCommon indications include displaced/unstable fractures, open fractures, vascular injury, compartment syndrome and failure of conservative treatment.\n\n* Intramedullary interlocking nail for tibia\n* ORIF with plate and screws in selected fractures\n* External fixation for severe open fractures and major soft-tissue injury\n* Bone grafting for selected delayed/non-unions\n* Early rehabilitation after stable fixation\n\n⸻\n\nDupuytren’s Fracture\n\nImportant examination correction: Dupuytren’s fracture is not the usual name for a shaft fracture of both tibia and fibula. It traditionally refers to an ankle injury involving a proximal fibular fracture with disruption of the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis and associated medial-sided ankle injury, historically related to Maisonneuve-type injury patterns.\n\nIf your university syllabus specifically lists “Both bones fracture of tibia and fibula – Dupuytren’s”, write the above tibia/fibula fracture answer and add:\n\nDupuytren’s fracture\n\n* Usually produced by external rotation and abduction forces at the ankle.\n* Associated with disruption of the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis.\n* May be associated with medial malleolar or deltoid ligament injury.\n* Clinical features include ankle pain, swelling, tenderness and difficulty bearing weight.\n* Management depends on stability; unstable syndesmotic injuries generally require reduction and fixation./////////////////Dislocations of Lower Limb—-\n\nAnterior, Posterior and Central Dislocation of Hip\nMechanism of Injury\n\n* Usually caused by high-energy trauma, especially road-traffic accidents.\n* Posterior dislocation: hip flexed and adducted, with a force directed along the femur, classically when the knee strikes the dashboard.\n* Anterior dislocation: forced abduction and external rotation of the hip.\n* Central dislocation: severe axial force directed through the femoral head, causing it to be driven medially through the acetabulum, usually with an acetabular fracture. \n\n⸻\n\n1. Anterior Dislocation of Hip\n\nDefinition\n\nDisplacement of the femoral head anteriorly from the acetabulum.\n\nMechanism\n\n* Forceful abduction + external rotation.\n* May occur with extension or flexion depending on the subtype.\n* Commonly occurs in RTA or falls. \n\nTypes\n\n1. Superior/anterior (pubic)\n2. Inferior/anterior (obturator)\n\nClinical Features\n\n* Severe hip pain\n* Inability to bear weight\n* Limb usually lies in abduction and external rotation\n* Superior type: hip is relatively extended\n* Inferior type: hip is flexed and abducted\n* Femoral head may be palpable anteriorly\n* Limb may appear shortened\n* Restricted and painful hip movements\n* Neurovascular examination is essential. \n\nComplications\n\n* Femoral nerve/artery/vein injury\n* Femoral head fracture\n* Acetabular fracture\n* Avascular necrosis of femoral head\n* Post-traumatic osteoarthritis\n* Recurrent instability\n* Sciatic nerve injury is less common than in posterior dislocation. \n\nManagement\n\n* Immediate trauma assessment and neurovascular examination\n* X-ray to confirm dislocation and identify associated fracture\n* Urgent closed reduction under adequate sedation/anesthesia\n* Post-reduction X-ray/CT to confirm concentric reduction and identify associated injuries\n* Open reduction if irreducible, unstable or associated with fractures requiring fixation. \n\n⸻\n\n2. Posterior Dislocation of Hip\n\nDefinition\n\nDisplacement of the femoral head posteriorly from the acetabulum. It is the most common type of traumatic hip dislocation. \n\nMechanism\n\n* Hip is flexed, adducted and internally rotated.\n* Sudden force is transmitted along the femur.\n* Classical example: dashboard injury during an RTA. \n\nClinical Features\n\n* Severe hip pain\n* Inability to bear weight\n* Limb is:\n    * Flexed\n    * Adducted\n    * Internally rotated\n    * Shortened\n* Prominent greater trochanter\n* Femoral head may be palpable posteriorly\n* Severe restriction of hip movements\n* Assess sciatic nerve function. \n\nComplications\n\n* Sciatic nerve injury\n* Avascular necrosis of femoral head\n* Femoral head fracture\n* Acetabular fracture\n* Post-traumatic osteoarthritis\n* Recurrent dislocation\n* Chondral injury\n* Associated knee injuries. \n\nManagement\n\nConservative/closed\n\n* Urgent closed reduction under adequate sedation/anesthesia.\n* Allis manoeuvre is commonly used for posterior dislocation.\n* Reassess neurovascular status after reduction.\n* Post-reduction X-ray and usually CT to detect associated fractures or intra-articular fragments.\n* Protected weight bearing and rehabilitation according to associated injuries.\n\nSurgical\n\n* Open reduction when:\n    * Closed reduction fails\n    * Dislocation is irreducible\n    * Reduction is non-concentric\n    * Associated femoral head/neck or acetabular fracture requires fixation\n    * Hip remains unstable after reduction. \n\n⸻\n\n3. Central Dislocation of Hip\n\nDefinition\n\nCentral dislocation is a medial displacement of the femoral head through a fractured acetabulum. It is therefore essentially a fracture-dislocation of the acetabulum, rather than a simple dislocation.\n\nMechanism\n\n* Severe axial loading transmitted through the femoral shaft.\n* Fall from height\n* RTA\n* Direct blow to the greater trochanter\n* Force drives the femoral head medially into/through the acetabular floor.\n\nClinical Features\n\n* Severe hip pain\n* Inability to bear weight\n* Marked restriction of hip movements\n* Swelling around hip\n* Limb may appear shortened\n* Abnormal position of femoral head may be difficult to appreciate clinically\n* Associated pelvic injuries may be present\n* Neurovascular assessment is essential.\n\nComplications\n\n* Acetabular fracture\n* Pelvic/visceral injury\n* Blood loss\n* Sciatic nerve injury\n* Avascular necrosis\n* Post-traumatic osteoarthritis\n* Hip stiffness\n* Heterotopic ossification\n* Malunion/non-union of acetabular fracture\n\nManagement\n\nInitial\n\n* ATLS assessment\n* Analgesia\n* Neurovascular examination\n* X-ray pelvis/hip\n* CT scan to define acetabular fracture pattern.\n\nConservative\n\n* Skeletal traction may be used in selected stable, minimally displaced injuries.\n* Analgesics\n* Non-weight bearing\n* Physiotherapy and gradual mobilization.\n\nSurgical\n\n* Indicated for displaced acetabular fractures, incongruent hip joint or instability.\n* Open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF) of the acetabulum.\n* Associated femoral head/neck fractures are treated appropriately.\n* Total hip arthroplasty may be required in selected severe injuries or secondary arthritis. ///////////////////////////Fracture of Cervical Spine ——\n\nDefinition\n\nA cervical spine fracture is a break in the continuity of one or more cervical vertebrae, with or without associated ligament injury, dislocation or spinal cord injury. Cervical spine trauma may cause spinal cord compression and neurological deficit, including quadriplegia. \n\n⸻\n\nCauses\n\n* Road-traffic accidents\n* Fall from height\n* Diving into shallow water\n* Sports injuries\n* Direct blow to the head or neck\n* Hyperflexion or hyperextension injuries\n* Axial loading of the cervical spine\n* Crush injuries\n\n⸻\n\nMechanism of Injury\n\n1. Hyperflexion\n\n* Sudden forward bending of the neck.\n* May cause vertebral body compression, flexion-distraction injury or fracture-dislocation.\n* Can produce spinal cord injury.\n\n2. Hyperextension\n\n* Sudden backward bending of the neck.\n* Common in road-traffic accidents and falls.\n* May cause extension injuries and spinal cord compression.\n\n3. Axial compression\n\n* Force transmitted vertically through the head.\n* Common in diving accidents and falls onto the head.\n* May produce burst-type fractures.\n\n4. Rotation\n\n* Rotational force may produce facet injuries and fracture-dislocations.\n\n5. Combined mechanism\n\n* Flexion, extension, rotation and axial loading may occur together in high-energy trauma.\n\n⸻\n\nClinical Features\n\nLocal features\n\n* Severe neck pain\n* Cervical muscle spasm\n* Tenderness over cervical spine\n* Swelling\n* Restricted neck movement\n* Deformity or abnormal neck position\n\nNeurological features\n\nDepending on the level and severity of spinal cord injury:\n\n* Weakness or paralysis of limbs\n* Loss of sensation\n* Tingling/numbness\n* Loss of reflexes initially\n* Respiratory difficulty\n* Bladder and bowel dysfunction\n\nQuadriplegia\n\nQuadriplegia/tetraplegia is paralysis or significant impairment of motor and/or sensory function in the four limbs and trunk due to cervical spinal cord injury.\n\nHigh cervical injuries may cause:\n\n* Paralysis of all four limbs\n* Loss of sensation\n* Respiratory muscle paralysis\n* Loss of bladder and bowel control\n* Autonomic dysfunction\n\nThe neurological level and completeness of spinal cord injury should be assessed using the ASIA/ISNCSCI neurological examination. \n\n⸻\n\nManagement of Cervical Spine Fracture\n\n1. Initial Management\n\nA. Immobilization\n\nUntil cervical spine injury is excluded, maintain spinal precautions.\n\n* Keep patient supine.\n* Maintain manual in-line stabilization during handling and airway procedures.\n* Apply a properly fitted rigid cervical collar when indicated.\n* Avoid unnecessary neck movements.\n* Log-roll the patient while maintaining spinal alignment.\n* Assess airway, breathing and circulation.\n* Perform repeated neurological examinations.\n\nRigid cervical immobilization is particularly important when there is neurological deficit or suspected significant cervical injury. \n\nB. Investigation\n\n* Cervical spine CT scan is commonly used to identify fractures and alignment abnormalities.\n* MRI is useful for spinal cord, disc, ligament and soft-tissue abnormalities, particularly when neurological deficit is present. \n\n⸻\n\n2. Management for Stabilization\n\nConservative management\n\nUsed for stable fractures without significant displacement or neurological compromise.\n\n* Rigid cervical collar\n* Cervical orthosis/brace\n* Analgesics\n* Activity restriction\n* Regular radiographs/CT as required\n* Physiotherapy after adequate healing\n\nStable cervical compression fractures may be treated with a cervical collar for several weeks. \n\nHalo immobilization\n\n* Halo vest may provide rigid external immobilization.\n* Used in selected stable or reducible cervical injuries.\n* Requires careful monitoring for complications.\n\nSurgical stabilization\n\nIndications:\n\n* Unstable fracture\n* Significant displacement or deformity\n* Fracture-dislocation\n* Progressive neurological deficit\n* Spinal cord compression\n* Failure of conservative treatment\n* Significant ligamentous disruption\n\nProcedures:\n\n* Anterior cervical decompression and fusion\n* Posterior cervical fixation and fusion\n* Combined anterior and posterior stabilization when required\n* Removal of compressive bone/disc fragments when indicated\n\nThe choice of surgical approach depends on the fracture pattern, instability, neurological status and site of spinal cord compression. \n\n⸻\n\n3. Management of Quadriplegia\n\nImmediate management\n\n1. Maintain cervical immobilization and spinal alignment.\n2. Maintain a patent airway.\n3. Provide oxygen and ventilatory support when required.\n4. Monitor respiratory function continuously.\n5. Maintain adequate circulation and blood pressure.\n6. Perform complete neurological assessment.\n7. CT/MRI to identify spinal cord compression.\n8. Urgent spine/neurosurgical consultation.\n9. Surgical decompression and stabilization when indicated.\n10. Prevent secondary complications.\n\nPrevention of complications\n\n* Regular repositioning\n* Pressure sore prevention\n* Chest physiotherapy\n* Assisted coughing and respiratory care\n* DVT prophylaxis\n* Bladder and bowel management\n* Prevention of contractures\n* Passive ROM exercises\n* Physiotherapy and occupational therapy\n* Psychological and social rehabilitation\n\nPatients with cervical spinal cord injury may require prolonged multidisciplinary rehabilitation. \n\n⸻\n\n4. Clay-Shoveler’s Fracture\n\nDefinition\n\nA Clay-shoveler’s fracture is an avulsion fracture of the spinous process, most commonly involving C7, but it may involve C6 to T3. \n\nMechanism\n\n* Direct blow to the posterior neck\n* Sudden forceful flexion\n* Sudden muscular/ligamentous traction on the spinous process\n* Sports injuries\n* RTA\n\nClinical features\n\n* Sudden localized neck pain\n* Pain between the shoulder blades\n* Local tenderness\n* Swelling\n* Restricted cervical movements\n* Usually no neurological deficit\n\nComplications\n\n* Chronic neck pain\n* Neck stiffness\n* Rare neurological injury\n* Non-union may occasionally cause persistent pain\n\nManagement\n\nConservative\n\n* Rest\n* Analgesics/NSAIDs\n* Hard cervical collar for comfort\n* Gradual return to activity\n* Physiotherapy after pain subsides\n\nMost isolated clay-shoveler’s fractures are stable injuries and heal with conservative treatment. \n\nSurgical\n\n* Rarely required.\n* Excision of the painful non-united fragment may be considered in persistent symptomatic cases. \n\n⸻\n\n5. Hangman’s Fracture\n\nDefinition\n\nA Hangman’s fracture is a traumatic fracture through the bilateral pars interarticularis/neural arch of C2, often associated with traumatic C2-C3 spondylolisthesis. \n\nMechanism\n\n* Hyperextension\n* Axial loading\n* Often associated with high-energy trauma\n* Examples: RTA, fall from height, diving injury\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe neck pain\n* Neck stiffness\n* Tenderness\n* Restricted cervical movements\n* Possible C2-C3 deformity\n* Neurological deficit is relatively uncommon in typical injuries because the spinal canal may become widened. \n\nComplications\n\n* C2-C3 instability\n* Non-union\n* Malunion\n* Chronic neck pain\n* Neurological injury in severe displacement\n* Vertebral artery injury\n* Post-traumatic deformity\n\n⸻\n\nClassification of Hangman’s Fracture\n\nLevine-Edwards classification\n\nType I\n\n* Hyperextension and axial compression\n* Minimal displacement\n* Stable injury\n\nType II\n\n* Hyperextension and axial loading followed by flexion\n* Significant displacement and angulation\n* Unstable\n\nType IIA\n\n* Flexion-distraction injury\n* Significant angulation\n* Usually little horizontal displacement\n\nType III\n\n* Type I injury associated with bilateral C2-C3 facet dislocation\n* Highly unstable. \n\n⸻\n\nManagement of Hangman’s Fracture\n\nConservative\n\nFor stable/minimally displaced injuries:\n\n* Rigid cervical collar\n* Analgesics\n* Regular radiological follow-up\n* Halo immobilization for selected unstable but reducible injuries\n* Gradual mobilization after healing\n\nType I injuries can commonly be treated with a rigid cervical collar. \n\nSurgical\n\nIndications:\n\n* Significant displacement\n* Severe angulation\n* Failure of closed reduction\n* C2-C3 facet dislocation\n* Severe instability\n* Neurological compression\n* Failure of conservative treatment\n\nProcedures:\n\n* Posterior C2-C3 fixation and fusion\n* Anterior C2-C3 fusion in selected cases\n* Combined fixation for complex injuries ///////////////////Fractures of Thoracic and Lumbar Spine —\n\nDefinition\n\nA thoracic or lumbar spine fracture is a break in one or more vertebral structures of the thoracic or lumbar spine, with or without associated ligament, spinal cord or nerve-root injury.\n\nThoracolumbar fractures commonly occur around the T11-L2 region, which represents the transition between the relatively rigid thoracic spine and mobile lumbar spine. \n\n⸻\n\n1. Mechanism of Injury\n\nThoracolumbar fractures commonly result from:\n\n1. Flexion-compression\n\n* Sudden forward flexion of the spine.\n* Produces compression/wedge fractures.\n* Common after falls and road-traffic accidents.\n\n2. Axial compression\n\n* Vertical force transmitted through the spine.\n* Produces burst fractures with compression of the vertebral body and possible retropulsion into the spinal canal.\n\n3. Flexion-distraction\n\n* Excessive forward bending with distraction of the posterior elements.\n* Produces Chance fracture.\n* Classically associated with seat-belt injuries.\n\n4. Hyperextension\n\n* Forced backward bending.\n* May produce anterior column injury and extension-type fractures.\n\n5. Rotation/shear\n\n* High-energy rotational or translational forces.\n* May produce fracture-dislocation and severe spinal instability.\n\n⸻\n\n2. Common Thoracic and Lumbar Fractures\n\nA. Compression/Wedge Fracture\n\nDefinition\n\nCollapse of the anterior portion of the vertebral body, producing a wedge-shaped vertebra.\n\nCauses\n\n* Osteoporosis\n* Fall\n* RTA\n* Axial loading\n\nClinical features\n\n* Localized back pain\n* Tenderness\n* Muscle spasm\n* Increased pain with movement\n* Kyphotic deformity may develop\n* Usually no neurological deficit in a simple stable fracture\n\nManagement\n\nConservative\n\n* Analgesics\n* Short period of rest\n* Thoracolumbosacral orthosis (TLSO) in selected cases\n* Early mobilization\n* Physiotherapy\n* Osteoporosis management where appropriate\n\nSurgical\n\n* Rare in stable fractures.\n* Consider vertebral augmentation or fixation in selected patients with persistent severe pain, progressive deformity or instability.\n\n⸻\n\n3. Burst Fracture\n\nDefinition\n\nA burst fracture occurs when axial compression causes the vertebral body to fracture in multiple directions, potentially pushing bone fragments into the spinal canal.\n\nMechanism\n\n* Severe axial loading\n* Fall from height landing on feet\n* RTA\n* Diving injury\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe back pain\n* Local tenderness\n* Muscle spasm\n* Restricted spinal movements\n* Possible kyphosis\n* Neurological deficit may occur due to canal compromise.\n\nComplications\n\n* Spinal cord/conus injury\n* Nerve-root compression\n* Progressive kyphosis\n* Chronic pain\n* Neurological deterioration\n* Spinal instability\n\nConservative management\n\nFor stable fractures without neurological deficit:\n\n* Analgesics\n* TLSO brace in selected patients\n* Early mobilization\n* Physiotherapy\n* Serial radiographs\n\nSurgical management\n\nIndications include:\n\n* Significant instability\n* Progressive deformity\n* Neurological deficit with compression\n* Significant canal compromise in appropriate clinical settings\n* Failure of conservative treatment\n\nProcedures:\n\n* Posterior pedicle-screw fixation\n* Decompression when indicated\n* Vertebral reconstruction/corpectomy in selected severe injuries\n* Fusion when instability requires it.\n\nAO describes non-operative treatment for selected stable thoracolumbar injuries and operative stabilization for unstable patterns. (surgeryreference.aofoundation.org￼)\n\n⸻\n\n4. Chance Fracture\n\nDefinition\n\nA Chance fracture is a flexion-distraction injury extending horizontally through the vertebral body and posterior elements.\n\nMechanism\n\n* Sudden flexion-distraction\n* Classically caused by a lap seat belt during RTA.\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe back pain\n* Local tenderness\n* Paraspinal muscle spasm\n* Possible abdominal bruising/seat-belt sign\n* Neurological deficit may occur.\n\nComplications\n\n* Spinal instability\n* Kyphotic deformity\n* Chronic back pain\n* Neurological injury\n* Associated abdominal/visceral injury\n\nManagement\n\nConservative\n\n* Selected stable bony injuries with intact posterior ligamentous complex\n* TLSO brace\n* Analgesia\n* Regular radiological follow-up\n* Physiotherapy\n\nSurgical\n\n* Indicated for ligamentous disruption, significant displacement, instability, progressive deformity or neurological compromise.\n* Posterior pedicle-screw fixation and fusion is commonly used.\n\n⸻\n\n5. Fracture-Dislocation of Thoracolumbar Spine\n\nMechanism\n\n* High-energy trauma\n* Severe flexion, extension, rotation or shear forces\n* RTA\n* Fall from height\n\nClinical features\n\n* Severe back pain\n* Obvious deformity\n* Severe muscle spasm\n* Inability to move\n* Neurological deficit may be present\n\nComplications\n\n* Spinal cord/conus injury\n* Cauda equina syndrome\n* Permanent neurological deficit\n* Spinal instability\n* Deformity\n* Chronic pain\n\nManagement\n\nInitial\n\n* Full spinal immobilization\n* ABC assessment\n* Neurological examination\n* CT/MRI as indicated\n\nSurgical\n\n* Usually requires reduction and internal fixation with pedicle screws and rods, often with fusion.\n* Decompression if neural structures are compressed.\n* Postoperative rehabilitation.\n\nThese injuries are generally considered unstable and require specialist spinal management. (surgeryreference.aofoundation.org￼)\n\n⸻\n\n6. General Clinical Features of Thoracic and Lumbar Fractures\n\nLocal features\n\n* Severe localized back pain\n* Vertebral tenderness\n* Paraspinal muscle spasm\n* Swelling\n* Restricted spinal movements\n* Kyphotic deformity\n* Bruising\n\nNeurological features\n\nDepending on the level and severity:\n\n* Lower-limb weakness/paralysis\n* Numbness or paraesthesia\n* Altered reflexes\n* Sensory loss\n* Bladder and bowel dysfunction\n* Sexual dysfunction\n* Cauda equina/conus symptoms in lower thoracolumbar injuries\n\nAssociated injuries\n\nHigh-energy trauma may be associated with:\n\n* Rib fractures\n* Chest injury\n* Abdominal injury\n* Pelvic fractures\n* Long-bone fractures\n\n⸻\n\n7. General Management\n\nA. Initial Management\n\n1. Spinal precautions and immobilization\n2. ABC assessment\n3. Maintain adequate oxygenation and circulation\n4. Neurological examination\n5. Analgesia\n6. CT scan for bony injury\n7. MRI when neurological deficit, ligamentous injury or spinal cord compression is suspected.\n\n⸻\n\nB. Conservative Management\n\nIndicated mainly for stable fractures without neurological deficit.\n\n* Bed rest only for a short period when necessary\n* Analgesics\n* TLSO brace where appropriate\n* Early mobilization\n* Physiotherapy\n* Back and abdominal muscle strengthening\n* Gradual return to activities\n* Regular radiological follow-up\n\nProlonged bed rest should generally be avoided because it increases complications such as DVT, pressure sores and deconditioning.\n\n⸻\n\nC. Surgical Management\n\nIndications\n\n* Unstable fracture\n* Progressive kyphosis\n* Significant displacement\n* Neurological deficit due to compression\n* Spinal cord/conus/cauda equina compression\n* Disruption of posterior ligamentous complex\n* Fracture-dislocation\n* Failure of conservative treatment\n\nProcedures\n\n* Posterior pedicle-screw fixation\n* Posterior spinal fusion\n* Decompression/laminectomy when indicated\n* Anterior decompression and fusion in selected cases\n* Combined anterior and posterior fixation for severe injuries\n\n⸻\n\n8. Fracture of Coccyx\n\nDefinition\n\nA coccygeal fracture is a break in one or more segments of the coccyx, usually caused by a direct fall onto the buttocks.\n\nCauses\n\n* Fall directly on the buttocks\n* Fall from height\n* Direct blow\n* Childbirth\n* Occasionally repetitive trauma\n\nMechanism\n\n* Direct axial force on the coccyx\n* Sudden backward bending of the coccyx\n* Direct impact while sitting/falling.\n\nClinical Features\n\n* Pain over coccyx (coccydynia)\n* Local tenderness\n* Pain while sitting\n* Pain during rising from a chair\n* Pain during defecation\n* Pain during prolonged sitting\n* Bruising/swelling may be present\n* Pain on direct palpation\n\nComplications\n\n* Chronic coccydynia\n* Non-union\n* Malunion\n* Painful sitting\n* Difficulty with defecation\n* Rare rectal injury\n\n⸻\n\nConservative Management\n\nMost coccygeal fractures are treated conservatively.\n\n1. Analgesics/NSAIDs\n2. Avoid prolonged sitting.\n3. Use a coccygeal cushion or wedge cushion.\n4. Sit leaning slightly forward to reduce pressure on the coccyx.\n5. Ice during the acute phase.\n6. Stool softeners if painful defecation is present.\n7. Gradual return to normal activity.\n8. Physiotherapy for persistent symptoms.\n\nSurgical Management\n\nSurgery is rarely required.\n\n* Considered for severe persistent pain despite prolonged conservative treatment.\n* Coccygectomy may be performed in carefully selected patients with chronic refractory coccydynia.////////////////////FRACTURE OF RIB CAGE —\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nA fracture of the rib cage is a break in the continuity of one or more ribs or the sternum, usually caused by direct or indirect trauma. Severe chest trauma may be associated with pneumothorax, haemothorax, pulmonary contusion or flail chest.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. FRACTURE OF RIBS\r\n\r\nMechanism of Injury\r\n\r\n* Direct trauma: A blow to the chest causes the rib to bend and fracture at the site of impact.\r\n* Indirect trauma: Compression of the chest from the sides may fracture ribs at their point of maximum curvature.\r\n* RTA\r\n* Fall\r\n* Sports injury\r\n* Crush injury\r\n* Severe coughing may rarely cause stress fractures.\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\n\r\n* Severe localized chest pain\r\n* Pain aggravated by:\r\n    * Deep inspiration\r\n    * Coughing\r\n    * Sneezing\r\n    * Movement\r\n* Local tenderness over the fractured rib\r\n* Swelling and bruising\r\n* Painful and shallow breathing\r\n* Crepitus may be present\r\n* Reduced chest expansion on the affected side\r\n* Respiratory distress in severe injuries\r\n\r\nComplications\r\n\r\n* Pneumothorax\r\n* Haemothorax\r\n* Pulmonary contusion\r\n* Pneumonia\r\n* Atelectasis\r\n* Respiratory failure\r\n* Flail chest\r\n* Injury to underlying organs\r\n\r\nManagement\r\n\r\nConservative\r\n\r\n* Assess airway, breathing and circulation.\r\n* Analgesics, including multimodal analgesia when required.\r\n* Ice application during the acute phase.\r\n* Encourage deep breathing and coughing exercises.\r\n* Incentive spirometry where appropriate.\r\n* Chest physiotherapy.\r\n* Early mobilization.\r\n* Treat associated pneumothorax/haemothorax if present.\r\n\r\nImportant: Routine tight chest binding is avoided because it restricts ventilation and may increase pulmonary complications.\r\n\r\nSurgical\r\n\r\nUsually not required for uncomplicated rib fractures.\r\n\r\nIndications may include:\r\n\r\n* Severe chest wall instability\r\n* Flail chest in selected patients\r\n* Multiple severely displaced fractures\r\n* Persistent respiratory compromise\r\n* Failure of conservative treatment\r\n\r\nSurgical stabilization of rib fractures (SSRF) may be performed using plates and screws in selected patients.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. FRACTURE OF STERNUM\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nA fracture of the sternum is a break in the continuity of the breastbone, commonly occurring after high-energy blunt trauma.\r\n\r\nMechanism of Injury\r\n\r\n* Direct blow to anterior chest\r\n* RTA, particularly impact against steering wheel or restraint system\r\n* Fall onto the chest\r\n* Compression injury\r\n* Severe chest trauma\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\n\r\n* Severe anterior chest pain\r\n* Localized tenderness\r\n* Sw